1
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:41,920
CONVERSATION 1
Hello, Doctor.

2
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,420
Hello, please take a seat.

3
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:46,480
I have your test results here, and it's good news.

4
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:48,560
The blood test came back clear.

5
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:52,080
There is no indication of any digestive issues.

6
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:55,180
So then, why do I feel so poorly all the time?

7
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:57,760
It's probably due to overwork and stress.

8
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:59,840
No, it can't be.

9
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:03,340
I've always been working hard, but I've never felt stress.

10
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:06,420
Other people suffer and complain about that, but I don't.

11
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:08,060
It must be something else.

12
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:11,480
What you have just described is a common sentiment.

13
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:15,260
Many people who suffer from stress fail to recognize it.

14
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:18,500
You told me you often work long into the night, right?

15
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:20,880
Yes, most days in fact.

16
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:23,640
But I've been doing that for about 20 years now.

17
00:01:23,640 --> 00:01:25,380
That doesn't matter.

18
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:29,340
You could have been suffering from stress for 20 years without knowing it.

19
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:31,420
And now it's catching up to you.

20
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,420
But what about my feeling tired all the time, and not being able to sleep well at night?

21
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:39,320
Those are common consequences of stress.

22
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:43,420
And if you don't sleep well, then of course you will feel fatigued.

23
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,300
I'm going to prescribe some special sleeping pills for you.

24
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:50,760
They have a soft, gentle effect, and are made from natural ingredients.

25
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:56,020
So your stomach should tolerate them fine, and there shouldn't be any negative side effects.

26
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,220
Take one with your dinner, and come see me after a month.

27
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:02,620
If there is no improvement, I'll give you something stronger.

28
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:03,820
Thank you, Doctor.

29
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:04,960
That's not all.

30
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:06,880
You should try and work less.

31
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,660
Is there any way you can decrease your workload?

32
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:12,340
Um, I'd have to think about it.

33
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:15,880
I'm a restaurant manager, and this industry is very competitive.

34
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:18,880
There are many things to keep track of and stay on top of.

35
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:23,300
I recommend you think about delegating some responsibilities to someone else.

36
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:26,640
I'm not asking you to retire, just to slow down a bit.

37
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:34,880
It's for your own health.

38
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:53,440
Q1. What do we learn about the man from his test results?

39
00:02:53,440 --> 00:03:12,120
Q2. What does the woman think is a common phenomenon among many people?

40
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:29,580
Q3. What does the woman say she will do for the man?

41
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:49,540
Q4. What does the man say about the industry he is engaged in?

42
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:53,180
CONVERSATION 2
Today on People in the News, our guest is John Williams.

43
00:03:53,180 --> 00:04:00,800
The name may not sound familiar to you, but John was once an acclaimed basketball player.

44
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:05,640
John, you stunned fans by leaving the sport at just 25.

45
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:08,280
Why did you retire so early?

46
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:12,840
Meg, I loved being an athlete, but I didn't love being a celebrity.

47
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:20,920
I was in the limelight when I was still a high school student, and went professional right after high school graduation, which was a mistake.

48
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:25,300
I was a shy kid, and I wasn't ready for all the media attention.

49
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:29,520
But walking away from millions of dollars at the height of your career?

50
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:33,940
Most people wouldn't be able to resist the lure of such a high salary.

51
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:40,640
When you left the sport, there was speculation that you were having issues with your teammates, or even an injury.

52
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:41,780
Not at all.

53
00:04:41,780 --> 00:04:43,280
It was hard to quit.

54
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:48,400
I was tempted to stay in the game, because I loved basketball, and I loved my team.

55
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:57,000
As for money, I turned professional at 18, so I'd actually earned a lot and saved most of it, because I had great financial advisors.

56
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,960
I knew basketball wasn't a career with a lot of longevity for most players.

57
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:05,220
So I wanted to change careers while I was still young.

58
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:09,300
Okay, that was 20 years ago, and you're back in the news.

59
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:14,200
You've created a foundation that works to get more kids playing team sports.

60
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:14,540
Why?

61
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:25,960
I went to university, and I studied public health and learned about the seriousness of the obesity epidemic, particularly among kids and adolescents in poor communities.

62
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:29,720
I've spent the last two decades trying to alleviate the problem.

63
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:32,420
The Foundation is just the latest attempt.

64
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:39,500
The Foundation uses private donations to support basketball teams for girls and boys in primary school, right?

65
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,580
Actually, we support teams for secondary school students, too.

66
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:53,660
And also have some public funding.

67
00:05:53,660 --> 00:06:12,480
Q5. Why did John Williams leave the sport of basketball at just 25?

68
00:06:12,480 --> 00:06:30,580
Q6. What does the man say about basketball as a career for most players?

69
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:49,240
Q7. What do we learn from the woman about John Williams 20 years later?

70
00:06:49,240 --> 00:07:37,240
Q8. What has the man spent the last two decades trying to do?

71
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:42,000
PASSAGE 1
Kate Atkinson was born in York, England in 1951.

72
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,340
She worked hard to gain her credentials as an author.

73
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:49,260
She studied English literature at University in Scotland.

74
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:55,620
After graduating in 1974, she researched a doctorate on American literature.

75
00:07:55,620 --> 00:08:03,060
Later, she taught at the university she graduated from, and began writing short stories in 1981.

76
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:11,020
She began writing for women's magazines after winning the 1986 Women's Own Short Story Competition.

77
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:18,740
Her first novel, Behind the Scenes at the Museum, won the 1995 UK Book of the Year award.

78
00:08:18,740 --> 00:08:24,640
The book is set in Yorkshire and has been adapted for radio, theatre, and TV.

79
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:28,480
She has written two plays for a theatre in Edinburgh.

80
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:37,320
The first was called Abandonment and the second, Nice, performed as part of the Edinburgh Festival in August 2000.

81
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:44,700
Whatever genre Atkinson writes in, her books touch on the themes of love and loss and how to carry on.

82
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:47,860
They are always presented with ingenuity and a wicked sense of humor.

83
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:59,660
Her books tend to be populated by odd, sometimes sinful, and generally flawed eccentrics who become credible by virtue of being so fully realized.

84
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:03,420
Her books have frequently been described as comedies of manners.

85
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:15,680
That is to say, comedies that represent the complex and sophisticated code of behavior current in fashionable circles of society, where appearances count more than true moral character.

86
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:24,600
A comedy of manners tends to reward its clever and deceitful characters, rather than punish their bad deeds.

87
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:38,084
The humor of a comedy of manners relies on verbal wit and playful teasing.

88
00:09:38,084 --> 00:09:55,420
Q9. When did Kate Atkinson begin writing for women's magazines?

89
00:09:55,420 --> 00:10:11,780
Q10. What common themes do Kate Atkinson's books touch on?

90
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:31,640
Q11. What does the passage say about the characters in a comedy of manners?

91
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:36,480
PASSAGE 2
Why is adaptability an important skill to exercise in the workplace?

92
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:43,240
Simply put, adaptability is a skill employers are increasingly looking for.

93
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:48,880
When you spend time learning a new task rather than resisting it, your productivity goes up.

94
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:57,340
You can also serve as an example to your coworkers who may be having trouble adapting, and can help lead your team forward.

95
00:10:57,340 --> 00:11:15,800
Strategy consultant, Dorie Clark, explains it to us this way: "I'd say that adaptability is an important skill in the workplace because, frankly, circumstances change—competitors introduce new products, the economy might enter a recession, customer preferences differ over time, and more.

96
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:19,440
If you shake your fist at the sky and say, 'why can't it stay the same?!'

97
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:21,960
, that's not going to do very much good.

98
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:31,320
Instead, you need to recognize when circumstances have changed so you can take appropriate action based on what is, rather than how you wish the world would be.

99
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:36,620
That enables you to make more accurate, informed, and effective choices."

100
00:11:36,620 --> 00:11:39,960
Also, the workplace itself has been evolving.

101
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:46,420
Today's work culture and management style is often based on teamwork, rather than a rigid hierarchy.

102
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:53,460
Brainstorming, which requires creativity, flexibility, and emotional intelligence, is a typical problem-solving technique.

103
00:11:53,460 --> 00:12:01,820
Employees who are unable or unwilling to participate will not easily move forward in the company.

104
00:12:01,820 --> 00:12:05,240
Employees who are flexible demonstrate other skills too.

105
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:13,000
They can reprioritize quickly when changes occur and suggest additional modifications when something is not working.

106
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:28,560
They can also regroup quickly when a setback occurs, adapting to the new situation confidently and without overreacting.

107
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:52,200
Q12. What does the speaker say about adaptability as a workplace skill?

108
00:12:52,200 --> 00:13:11,000
Q13. According to Dorie Clark, what does recognizing changed circumstances enable people to do?

109
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:28,940
Q14. What does the passage say about today's work culture?

110
00:13:28,940 --> 00:14:14,940
Q15. What are employees with adaptability able to do when changes occur?

111
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:18,600
RECORDING 1
What makes humans different from other species?

112
00:14:18,600 --> 00:14:26,740
Some philosophers argue it's morals or ethics, while some scientists assert it's our greater cognitive development.

113
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:31,760
But I argue that the main difference is our desire to combat routine.

114
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:42,840
This makes being creative a biological mandate, as what we seek in art and technology is surprise, not simply a fulfillment of expectations.

115
00:14:42,840 --> 00:15:06,240
As a result, a wild imagination has characterized the history of our species: we build intricate habitats, devise complex recipes for our food, wear clothes that reflect constant changes in fashion, communicate with elaborate signs, symbols and sounds, and travel between habitats on wings and wheels of our own design.

116
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:11,640
To satisfy our appetite for novelty, innovation is key.

117
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:13,740
But who innovates?

118
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:20,260
Now, many people, both laymen and experts, believe that only geniuses innovate.

119
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:25,020
But I believe that innovation is not something that only a few people do.

120
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:36,560
The innovative drive lives in every human brain, and the resulting war against the repetitive is what powers the massive changes that distinguish one generation from the next.

121
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:40,640
The drive to create the new is a trait of being human.

122
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:45,300
We build cultures by the hundreds and tell new stories by the millions.

123
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:52,800
We create and surround ourselves with things that have never existed before, while animals do not.

124
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:55,620
But where do our new ideas come from?

125
00:15:55,620 --> 00:16:01,520
According to many, new ideas come from seemingly nowhere, to great minds.

126
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:05,620
From this perspective, new ideas are almost like magic.

127
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:09,660
They come in a flash of inspiration to a select few.

128
00:16:09,660 --> 00:16:18,540
However, the reality is that, across the spectrum of human activities, prior work propels the creative process.

129
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:28,520
We may think of innovation as being the result of inspiration or genius, but it's really the result of developing the ideas of others further.

130
00:16:28,520 --> 00:16:34,480
This happens in technology where one invention enables or inspires further inventions.

131
00:16:34,480 --> 00:16:43,100
And it happens in the arts, as writers, composers, and painters use the work of previous artists in their own work.

132
00:16:43,100 --> 00:16:46,140
The human brain works from precedent.

133
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:50,880
We take the ideas we've inherited and put them together into some new shape.

134
00:16:50,880 --> 00:16:52,900
What is a true creator?

135
00:16:52,900 --> 00:16:56,660
Is a creator a genius who makes something out of nothing?

136
00:16:56,660 --> 00:17:06,480
No. Creators are simply humans who use what they inherit who absorb the past and manipulate it to create possible futures.

137
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:18,023
Thus, humans are creators as a rule rather than as an exception.

138
00:17:18,023 --> 00:17:37,260
Q16. What is the main question the speaker is trying to answer?

139
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:57,160
Q17. What does the speaker say about innovation?

140
00:17:57,160 --> 00:18:13,600
Q18. According to the speaker, where do new ideas actually come from?

141
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:25,420
RECORDING 2
Many dog owners will tell you that their dog somehow knows when they're ill or upset, and, according to researchers who study dog cognition, those pet owners are right.

142
00:18:25,420 --> 00:18:30,640
Dogs do know when their human companions are having a rough time.

143
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:42,400
Not only can your dog sense when you have a cold, but domestic dogs have shown an aptitude for detecting both much smaller mood fluctuations and far more serious physical conditions.

144
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:49,100
This is because dogs are extremely sensitive to changes in the people they're familiar with, and illness causes change.

145
00:18:49,100 --> 00:19:04,900
If a person is infected with a virus or bacteria, for example, their odour will be abnormal, and dogs are able to smell that change even if a human can't, because dogs have a much more powerful sense of smell than humans.

146
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:13,340
Researchers have also found that a person's mood, which can be an indicator of a larger illness, triggers a dog's sense of smell.

147
00:19:13,340 --> 00:19:22,160
Human emotions manifest physically in chemical signals that are emitted by the body, and dogs are able to smell those as well.

148
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:28,080
Beyond smell, dogs gather information from a person's voice in order to sense changes.

149
00:19:28,080 --> 00:19:44,460
In 2014, researchers discovered that dogs have an area of the brain, similar to one in humans, that allows them to understand emotional cues in the tone of a speaker's voice, beyond what they'd be able to pick up from familiar words alone.

150
00:19:44,460 --> 00:19:48,820
A person's voice can also carry indicators of illness.

151
00:19:48,820 --> 00:19:54,300
What's not understood quite so well is what dogs understand about these changes.

152
00:19:54,300 --> 00:20:01,200
Humans send out lots of cues, but whether dogs know some of these cues mean "illness" isn't clear.

153
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:14,840
What we perceive as concern on a dog's part might be more like increased curiosity or suspicion that something is wrong with us, and sticking close by is a great way to gather more information about the situation.

154
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:29,400
Some researchers assert dogs will one day help doctors diagnose diseases, as some dogs have already demonstrated the ability to detect an assortment of ailments, including diabetes and certain types of cancer.

155
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:33,420
But those researchers concede that's probably in the distant future.

156
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:40,160
For now, research suggests dog ownership can have an array of benefits in and of itself.

157
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:44,740
Keeping a pet dog has been shown to bolster health and boost mood.

158
00:20:44,740 --> 00:20:58,220
Dogs also help people relax, and they can be a particular comfort to those with chronic diseases.

159
00:20:58,220 --> 00:21:19,000
Q19. What do researchers who study dog cognition confirm?

160
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:38,300
Q20. Why are dogs able to detect abnormal odours caused by illness?

161
00:21:38,300 --> 00:21:58,520
Q21. What does the passage say about dog ownership?

162
00:21:58,520 --> 00:22:10,660
RECORDING 3
Earlier this month, the think-tank called Onward published a report, "A Question of Degree", which argues that degrees in the creative arts are not good value for money.

163
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:29,900
Ministers, according to Onward, should "crack down on courses that offer extremely limited value for money to students ten years after graduation", restricting the ability of such courses to recruit new students, if the average graduate earns below the student loans payment threshold.

164
00:22:29,900 --> 00:22:38,260
Courses like science, technology, engineering, and math, and economics, where the average graduate earns a lot, should be favored.

165
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:47,960
The report provides insight into a government review which looks at how to reform technical education and how to ensure students get good value for money.

166
00:22:47,960 --> 00:22:53,260
At first glance, it might even seem like Onward have a point.

167
00:22:53,260 --> 00:23:02,240
According to their data, the majority of creative arts students earn less than £25,000 a year, ten years after graduation.

168
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:10,960
The average male creative arts students, indeed, apparently earn much less than they would, had they simply never gone to university.

169
00:23:10,960 --> 00:23:22,780
This isn't really good for anyone—and it's certainly no good for graduates, who are forced to endure a lifestyle where they can never save up, never buy a house, never hope to retire.

170
00:23:22,780 --> 00:23:25,920
Onward have identified a real problem.

171
00:23:25,920 --> 00:23:44,580
Creative arts graduates from top universities like Oxford, with a high proportion of privately-educated students, have fairly good work prospects, while 40 percent of all graduates—regardless of their degree—are on less than £25,000 a year, five years after graduation.

172
00:23:44,580 --> 00:23:54,320
This suggests that the problem isn't really to do with specific students studying specific degrees, but really with the economy as a whole.

173
00:23:54,320 --> 00:24:02,580
Regardless of what they've studied, young people find it hard to get ahead, unless they're lucky enough to be born with successful parents.

174
00:24:02,580 --> 00:24:10,900
If ministers want to make education pay for young people, they need to look beyond the higher education sector, towards the wider world.

175
00:24:10,900 --> 00:24:20,480
The rewards that education gives us are not measurable—they are not always instantly obvious, and certainly not always direct.

176
00:24:20,480 --> 00:24:25,860
An education makes you a different person from the one you would have been if you hadn't received it.

177
00:24:25,860 --> 00:24:31,780
We need to look at the value of education not in the context of a bank balance, but of a life.

178
00:24:31,780 --> 00:24:47,280
If we continue to allow ourselves to be distracted with talk of "value for money", we will all be made poorer as a result.

179
00:24:47,280 --> 00:25:06,320
Q22. What does the Onward report argue?

180
00:25:06,320 --> 00:25:21,640
Q23. What does the speaker think of Onward's argument?

181
00:25:21,640 --> 00:25:44,040
Q24. What do we learn about British college graduates five years after graduation?

182
00:25:44,040 --> 00:26:04,821
Q25. What does the speaker say actually accounts for the problem identified by Onward?
